bellum  slavery not just in the United States  but in the entire hemisphere you're  talking about maybe four million folks  who were brought from Africa during the  the slave trade whether to Brazil  whether to the United States what's now  the United States the Caribbean Basin  etc so the numbers are greater but of  course there's a lot more people in the  world I think one of the things that's  different now though is that entire  societies are not based on this  everywhere in the world pretty much  everywhere in the world slavery is  illegal now that wasn't the case in the  Gulf states it wasn't the case in Saudi  Arabia within living memory and so those  countries or places like Mauritania and  Mali who are just now making it illegal  the age-old practices of hereditary  slavery they're at a very different  place than most other countries but what  most countries are having to do is 100  or 150 years after they abolished legal  slavery they're having to confront its  presence in their society so there's  that moment I think for policy makers  around the world where everybody says oh  my god it's happening again right or  maybe it never stopped happening we just  stopped looking for it and that's I  think you hit the nail on the head there  because I mean though that's a 25  million that's a huge number all around  the world and even in the United States  it's happening and you people say how  can that possibly be happening in this  country  so shed some light on that how is it  happening in the United States well it  happens even in countries like the  United States where there is rule of law  and where corruption is not the norm and  where the economy no longer depends on  it like the southern agricultural  economy depended on it up until about  the 1940s that's not the case anymore  but the reason why I think there's these  echoes of slavery and you see people who  are being subjected to practices that  directly violate the Thirteenth  Amendment even today is 