ink more because they needed there  weren't that many spanish-speaking  attorneys as you got more people from  the Asian and Latino communities coming  into the government and then  in roles like this I think a lot of us  came in and we looked at things with  they're fresh and new lens you know  saying you know this is happening on our  communities because we know it from  talking to our cousins and just knowing  our own history and so there was kind of  an insistence I think on the part of a  lot of that Latino and especially Asian  and South Asian lawyers that this is  something that we wanted to work on as  well as working on quote-unquote  traditional civil rights and do you  think the problem of human trafficking  and slavery is on the rise or do you  think it's being held at bay you know  it's hard for me to tell I mean I think  that one of the things that we're seeing  is that there's no good numbers the  prevalence numbers are people are  working very hard to try to get this  good social scientists many of whom are  now finally starting to look not at  things that are biased by enforcement  numbers enforcement numbers could be  totally skewed simply by what the  priorities of that local law enforcement  office is if if they have somebody to  speak Spanish then their numbers will  end up having a bunch of cases that have  spanish-speaking victims and if they  don't they may never actually identify  those cases so there's some new  prevalence work that's being done by  sociologists and anthropologists around  the world that are trying to use some of  the predictive things that are coming  out of the war crimes and genocide space  and that's really interesting we haven't  really started to kind of bridge the  gaps from Gilder Lehrman Center which is  up till now been I think much more of  kind of a History Center it's very much  the mission but at the same time I think  we're starting to see now with some of  the different fellows that have come  through to the GLC recently and with  some of the work that we're hoping to do  in the coming years very much bridging  the gap over to the sociologists the  anthropologists the business school I  guess here it's the school of management  as well as even the folks in from the  public health over at the Medical School  I think there's a lot of ways that we  can start to put together that knowledge  base so that we can answer that question  in the future a much more  interdisciplinary kind of approach to  solve the problem exactly  but prevalence aside you know one of the  things you know Bill Gates famously said  a couple of other donors in the  anti-trafficking space that he'd take a  pass on doing work in this and work on  malaria and other things because he  didn't feel like this was countable yet  and of course that's the problem it's a  hidden crime people's suffering in  silence that don't come forward and  unlike domestic violence or sexual  violence we haven't necessarily come up  with the proxies yet so that we can come  up with a number to answer people like  gates and others to say yes we can  measure this and therefore we can see  whether it's up down sideways and  whether any particular intervention  really is making a difference across the  board what we do know though is that the  individual intervention that you make  when somebody goes out through a window  and seeks help if you have the policies  in place so that they aren't going from  the firing pan into the fire so that  they're incorporated into government  protections into nongovernmental  organizations service providers even  though that's a one-off that's not a  systemic fix you know to me there is  that that you know kind of saying that  everybody has as far as you know in each  individual life is the universe and I  think it's the same thing that you know  if we can do right by one trafficking  victim at a time eventually we can have  that universe of freedom right right I  am wanting to talk about the term victim  centered approach to modern slavery and  how it's different from what was  previously done so let's talk about that  a little bit well typically in most  legal systems including the United  States a person who suffers a crime is a  witness they were often called a  complaining witness so you talked about  your CW in law enforcement it's slang as  opposed to this idea that they're a  person who had been victimized by what  happened to them they were valuable more  for being for what they could tell you  about the crime as opposed to that  something had happened to them the Civil  Rights Division prosecutors I think came  from a little bit of a different place  because we were having to vindicate the  rights that had been violated it wasn't  that the interest of the United States  in not having houses burned down his was  being violated when somebody who would  do a racially motivated arson it was  that family's interest under the First  Amendment and Fourteenth Amendment in  being able to live in an integrated  neighborhood without being subjected to  violence and so I think our set point  without knowing that this was kind of  you know it's like asking a fish about  the water right our sweat point or our  set point was very much we start with  this person's rights were violated and I  think that out of that Q flowed the  victim centered approach which was you  know we are here for what's best for  them if it's a child victim and they  might not be able to testify because of  trauma we either come up with another  way to prosecute the case or we charge  something that's not really going to  require that child to have to testify we  have to bend to allow when I say we I  mean law enforcement has to bend to  allow nongovernmental organizations and  advocacy groups the church others to  come into the case and have a level of  power that you never would allow in an  organized crime case or a  money-laundering case or something like  that and so a lot of the victim centered  approach is kind of resetting the  criminal justice response so you really  are putting the victim and their needs  at the center it's not that you're  providing them housing and psychological  services simply to get them to be a  better witness for you it's you're  providing them housing and psychological  services because when you're trying to  put together somebody's life after their  rights have been violated and you as the  state are the guarantor of those rights  then it flows from that that you should  do those things to make them whole or  even better than they were before the  trafficker got them so I think that a  lot of the victim centered approach is  simply you know where you stand and look  and if you stand with the victims and  listen to them I think you end up having  that instrumentality of a better case  yes but more importantly the entire  ethos of the work that you're doing  changes right and and that makes sense  and I'm wondering how well that's been  accepted in this country it has in fits  and starts obviously just a few weeks  ago we saw the the commutation of  sentence of a young woman who had been  in Kentucky I think I had been in prison  for 15 years after killing a John a  client she was an underage girl who was  in prostitution therefore under the  American law and the UN was  automatically considered a trafficking  victim you don't have to show that  somebody's will is over born if they're  under 18 because just like with  statutory rape there's this idea that  somebody that age can't consent to be  involved in commercial sex and so go  back you know a couple of decades and  here's this fifteen-year-old girl who  ends up killing one of the men who's  raping her let's not use euphemisms as  to what the the clients of underage  people in prostitution are doing and I  think that we saw just you know whether  it was online or otherwise in much of  December and early January of this year  a real outpouring from people saying no  she should not be in jail for what she  did so I think that it's it's getting  out there in the culture I think that  that ethos is happening you know people  are not just being seen as oh that's an  illegal alien or that's a prostitute you  know one of the things that we heard at  the GL sees modern slavery conference in  November was just how for most of the  20th century even the FBI who was  supposed to be investigating these cases  would look at a case and say oh well you  know she's got a bad reputation in the  so we're not gonna bring the case it's  like well of course she has a bad  reputation in the community her pimp was  putting her out on the street through  force threats of force or because she  was underage in fact that's the case  that law enforcement should be running  towards rather than running away from I  think that that cultural shift is  happening but we have to keep the hammer  down right right so how do we shine the  light on those shadows on modern slavery  what are some things that need to happen  I think women to turn the culture and  make a difference I think one of the  things is to look at kind of what has  worked in the past one of the the most  effective things in the British Empire  as far as abolition was concerned were  the boycotts of sugar from the West  Indies and so you had consumer activism  at a time when people didn't even have  labels like consumer in the early 1800s  where people were actively shunning West  India and sugar now that created a  market for colonial sugar that was  coming from East India and in which you  know displacement effect there was a lot  of other abuses and and and things from  that but as far as what it did with that  particular slavery in the Caribbean is  is that it really started moving towards  the endgame when you had British  consumers who were making their  decisions with their pocketbooks it got  the attention of not only the sugar  growers but it certainly got the  attention of their 